+# Deploying the USDC Tip Jar
+
+The site ships as a static export pinned on IPFS and served through an ENS name. There is no server
+and no serverless function: every route is a prerendered HTML file, and all chain reads happen in
+the browser against a Base RPC.
+
+Nothing in this file has been run against mainnet or uploaded anywhere. It is the runbook.
+
+Replace `tipjar.yourname.eth` and `<ALCHEMY_KEY>` with your own throughout.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Prerequisites
+
+- `TipJar` deployed to Base mainnet (step 1) — the build will not compile without it.
+- An Alchemy (or equivalent) Base RPC key.
+- A WalletConnect project ID from <https://cloud.reown.com>.
+- An ENS name you control, on Ethereum mainnet.
+- Foundry's `cast` on your `PATH` for the verification commands.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Deploy the contract to Base
+
+The frontend reads contract addresses from `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts`, which
+is generated from deployment broadcasts. Until the jar exists on chain 8453, that file has no `8453`
+entry and `yarn build` fails with a TypeScript error like `Type '"TipJar"' is not assignable to type
+'"USDC"'`. **That error is the guard** — it means you are about to ship a frontend pointed at a
+chain where the jar does not exist. Do not work around it.
+
+```bash
+# From the repo root. Uses the keystore account, not a raw private key.
+yarn account:import          # once, if you have not imported a deployer yet
+yarn deploy --network base   # prints "TipJar deployed at 0x..."
+```
+
+Use plain `yarn deploy`, not `yarn deploy --file DeployTipJar.s.sol`. `Deploy.s.sol` already runs
+`DeployTipJar`, and `script/VerifyAll.s.sol` only reads `broadcast/Deploy.s.sol/<chainid>/` — deploy
+through the `--file` form and the verify step below cannot find the broadcast.
+
+Then verify the source on Basescan (needs `ETHERSCAN_API_KEY` in `packages/foundry/.env`):
+
+```bash
+yarn verify --network base
+```
+
+Confirm the generated file picked up the deployment:
+
+```bash
+grep -n "8453" packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts
+```
+
+Sanity-check the live jar before building a frontend for it:
+
+```bash
+JAR=0x...   # the address printed by yarn deploy
+BASE_RPC=https://base-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/<ALCHEMY_KEY>
+
+cast call $JAR "token()(address)" --rpc-url $BASE_RPC
+# must print 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+cast call $JAR "owner()(address)" --rpc-url $BASE_RPC
+```
+
+---
+
+## 2. Point the frontend at Base
+
+Edit `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts`:
+
+```diff
+-  targetNetworks: [chains.foundry],
++  targetNetworks: [chains.base],
+```
+
+This is a deliberate literal rather than an env switch: an env-driven ternary widens the chain ID to
+`number`, which collapses the contract-name and function-name type inference across the whole app —
+and that inference is what produces the guard described in step 1.
+
+`chains.foundry` is correct for local development even though the fork carries Base's state, because
+Anvil serves the fork under chain ID 31337. See the README.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Set the build environment
+
+Create `packages/nextjs/.env.local` (git-ignored) from `.env.example`:
+
+```bash
+NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY=<ALCHEMY_KEY>
+NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID=<WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID>
+NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_RPC_URL=https://base-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/<ALCHEMY_KEY>
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL=https://tipjar.yourname.eth.link
+```
+
+Why each one matters:
+
+| Variable                                | Without it                                                                     |
+| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL`             | `og:image` and the canonical URL point at `http://localhost:3000`               |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_RPC_URL`               | Base falls back to the shared Scaffold-ETH Alchemy key, which is rate-limited   |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID`  | WalletConnect sessions use a shared demo project ID                            |
+
+These are `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`, so they are baked into the JavaScript bundle at build time and are
+public. Never put a private key or a secret-bearing RPC URL in them.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Clean build
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+rm -rf .next out
+
+NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true \
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://tipjar.yourname.eth.link" \
+NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_RPC_URL="https://base-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/<ALCHEMY_KEY>" \
+  yarn build
+```
+
+`yarn ipfs:build` is exactly the `rm -rf .next out && NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true yarn build` part,
+if you prefer the short form and keep the rest in `.env.local`.
+
+Three things this relies on, all already configured — do not "simplify" them away:
+
+- **`NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true`** turns on `output: "export"`, `trailingSlash: true` and
+  `images.unoptimized` in `next.config.ts`. `trailingSlash` is the one that matters most: without
+  it Next emits `debug.html`, and an IPFS gateway resolves directories to `index.html` but will not
+  guess a bare filename, so `/debug` 404s. With it you get `debug/index.html`, which resolves.
+- **`NODE_OPTIONS="--require ./polyfill-localstorage.cjs"`** is baked into the `build` script in
+  `packages/nextjs/package.json`. Node 25 exposes a `localStorage` global that has no Web Storage
+  methods until a backing file is configured, so `next-themes` and RainbowKit feature-detect it and
+  then crash with `TypeError: localStorage.getItem is not a function` — a 500 in `next dev` and
+  `Error occurred prerendering page` during the build. Next prerenders in worker processes, so a
+  fix in `next.config.ts` or `instrumentation.ts` never reaches the crash; only `NODE_OPTIONS`,
+  which every child process inherits, does. A `--require` shim rather than
+  `--no-experimental-webstorage` because that flag only exists on Node 25 and this package supports
+  Node >= 20.18.3, where an unknown flag is a hard startup error.
+- **No `NEXT_PUBLIC_IGNORE_BUILD_ERROR=true`.** That flag makes `next.config.ts` swallow TypeScript
+  errors, including the step 1 guard. This project builds type-clean; if it does not, fix the cause.
+
+Scaffold-ETH's local block explorer is excluded from the export as
+`app/_blockexplorer-disabled/`. Its `[address]` and `[txHash]` routes cannot be statically exported
+and its pages touch `localStorage` at import time. If you re-enable it locally, rename it back
+before building.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Verify the build before uploading
+
+An unchanged CID after a deploy means the bytes were identical — either the build did not pick up
+your change, or you uploaded the wrong directory. Check both before you upload, not after.
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+
+# Every route is a directory with an index.html — this is the trailingSlash check.
+ls out/*/index.html
+# expect: out/404/index.html  out/debug/index.html  out/_not-found/index.html
+test -f out/index.html && echo "home page ok"
+
+# og:image is absolute and is not localhost.
+grep -o 'property="og:image"[^>]*' out/index.html
+# expect: content="https://tipjar.yourname.eth.link/thumbnail.png"
+! grep -rq "localhost:3000" out/ && echo "no localhost leaked into the build"
+
+# The custom RPC actually made it into the bundle (and the shared key did not sneak back in).
+grep -rl "base-mainnet.g.alchemy.com" out/_next/static/chunks/ | head -1
+
+# The build is newer than the sources it was built from.
+stat -c '%y  %n' app/page.tsx app/_components/*.tsx out/
+# If any source timestamp is newer than out/, the build is stale. Go back to step 4.
+
+# The jar address you deployed in step 1 is the one in the bundle (addresses are lowercased there).
+JAR=0x...
+grep -rl "$(echo "$JAR" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')" out/_next/static/chunks/ | head -1
+```
+
+Then open it locally exactly as a gateway would serve it:
+
+```bash
+(cd out && python3 -m http.server 3003) &
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L http://127.0.0.1:3003/
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L http://127.0.0.1:3003/debug/
+# Then open http://127.0.0.1:3003 in a browser and click through: connect, approve, tip.
+kill %1
+```
+
+---
+
+## 6. Upload to IPFS
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+yarn bgipfs upload config init -u https://upload.bgipfs.com   # once per machine
+yarn bgipfs upload out
+```
+
+Or, from the repo root, `yarn ipfs` — which runs the clean build, the config init and the upload in
+one go, and prints the review URL.
+
+**Save the CID.** Everything below refers to it.
+
+```bash
+CID=bafy...
+```
+
+---
+
+## 7. Review the upload
+
+```bash
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://community.bgipfs.com/ipfs/$CID/"
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://community.bgipfs.com/ipfs/$CID/debug/"
+curl -s -L "https://community.bgipfs.com/ipfs/$CID/" | grep -o 'property="og:image"[^>]*'
+```
+
+Then open `https://community.bgipfs.com/ipfs/$CID/` in a browser and walk the whole flow: connect a
+wallet, confirm the network prompt says Base, approve, tip, watch the feed update, reject a
+transaction and confirm the UI recovers.
+
+> **Gateway styles.** Next emits root-absolute asset paths (`/_next/static/...`). Under a *subdomain*
+> gateway (`https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/`) or at an ENS root those resolve correctly. Under a raw
+> *path* gateway that does not redirect to a subdomain, the browser asks for `https://gateway/_next/...`
+> and gets a 404, so the page loads unstyled. Most public gateways redirect `/ipfs/$CID` to the
+> subdomain form, which is why the URLs above work — if yours does not, review at
+> `https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/` instead. This is a gateway property, not a problem with the build.
+
+Get the CID reviewed and approved before touching ENS. Setting a content hash is a mainnet
+transaction; pointing it at a broken build costs gas twice.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Point the ENS name at the CID
+
+Two mainnet transactions, both through <https://app.ens.domains>.
+
+**First time only — create the subname.** Open `https://app.ens.domains/yourname.eth` → *Subnames* →
+*New subname* → enter `tipjar` → *Next* → skip the profile → *Open Wallet* → confirm. If the
+transaction stalls, switch MetaMask to Ethereum, open *Activity*, and use *Speed up*.
+
+**Every release — set the content hash.** Open `https://app.ens.domains/tipjar.yourname.eth` →
+*Records* → *Edit Records* → *Other* → put `ipfs://<CID>` in the Content Hash field → *Save* →
+confirm in your wallet.
+
+For an update, only this second transaction is needed.
+
+---
+
+## 9. Verify after deploying
+
+```bash
+NAME=tipjar.yourname.eth
+CID=bafy...
+ETH_RPC=https://eth.llamarpc.com
+
+# 1. The content hash is onchain and decodes to your CID.
+RESOLVER=$(cast call 0x00000000000C2e074eC69A0dFb2997BA6C7d2e1e \
+  "resolver(bytes32)(address)" $(cast namehash $NAME) --rpc-url $ETH_RPC)
+cast call $RESOLVER "contenthash(bytes32)(bytes)" $(cast namehash $NAME) --rpc-url $ETH_RPC
+# The tail of the returned bytes is your CID, hex-encoded with an 0xe3010170 ipfs-ns prefix.
+
+# 2. The gateway serves it. Allow 5-15 minutes for caches to catch up.
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$NAME.link/"
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$NAME.link/debug/"
+
+# 3. Unfurl metadata resolves to the live domain, not localhost.
+curl -s -L "https://$NAME.link/" | grep -o 'property="og:\(image\|title\|description\)"[^>]*'
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$NAME.link/thumbnail.png"
+```
+
+Use `.eth.link` rather than `.eth.limo` — it behaves better on mobile.
+
+Finally, in a browser at `https://tipjar.yourname.eth.link`:
+
+- The tab title is *USDC Tip Jar*, not *Scaffold-ETH 2*.
+- Connecting on the wrong chain offers to switch to Base, and switching works.
+- Approve, then tip, with a small real amount. The stats and the feed update.
+- Rejecting a transaction in the wallet leaves the UI usable.
+- Addresses render as ENS names or blockies, never raw hex.
+- Pasting the URL into Slack, X or Telegram unfurls with the tip jar image.
+
+---
+
+## Rolling back
+
+CIDs are immutable, so the previous build is still pinned. Repeat step 8's second transaction with
+the previous CID. No rebuild needed — keep a short log of shipped CIDs:
+
+| Date | CID | Contract (Base) | Notes |
+| ---- | --- | --------------- | ----- |
+|      |     |                 |       |
+
+---
+
+## Alternative: Vercel
+
+IPFS is the recommended path — it avoids Vercel's build memory limits and keeps the site
+decentralized. If you need Vercel anyway, this is a monorepo, so configure the project as:
+
+- **Root Directory:** `packages/nextjs`
+- **Install Command:** `cd ../.. && yarn install`
+- **Build Command:** leave default
+- **Output Directory:** leave default
+
+and set the same `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variables in the project's environment. Do **not** set
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD` there — Vercel serves the app, so the static export is unnecessary. Common
+failures: *"No Next.js version detected"* means the root directory is not set; a build command
+containing `cd` conflicts with the root directory; an OOM / exit code 129 means the monorepo
+exceeded the build memory limit, so build locally and push with `vercel --prebuilt`, or use IPFS.
